#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Answer when a Scripture locus was written and when what it tells of happened.

Usage: scripture-chronology {validate,query,coverage,build,check} [--json]

The corpus under src/sources/chronology holds typed temporal assertions keyed
to canonical Scripture loci: composition, narrated event, utterance, historical
and superscription setting, retrospective event, prophecy given, and prophetic
referent. One event is dated once and bound from every locus that needs it, so
parallel passages cannot acquire parallel dates. guidance/scripture-chronology.md
owns the contract and scripts/_chronology.py is the whole seam.

This is its own tool rather than a verb on an existing one because its question
is its own. Every neighbouring tool answers where a text is, how it is numbered,
or what is printed at it; none of them answers when, and giving one of them a
second question is the thing the shared launcher's own listing exists to
prevent. The commentary edge is the precedent: a new tracked data family beside
the source library, with its own guidance and its own registered tool.

`validate` collects every problem rather than stopping at the first, because an
author fixing authored data wants the list. `check` refuses a stale generated
table instead of rebuilding it, because a verification that repairs what it was
meant to detect reports success either way.

Requires PyYAML (requirements-tools.txt).
"""
from __future__ import annotations

